  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Thursday)\nIssued at 305 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nCurrent upper air analysis and water vapor imagery shows \nshortwave ridging over the region with moisture associated with a \ndeveloping wave over the northern Rockies. It's been a dry and \nmild day across western SD and northeastern WY with a few high \nclouds moving overhead. \n\nAn upper low will pass to the north of the CWA tomorrow with an \nattendant cold front pushing through the area tomorrow morning. \nThis front will bring breezy west to northwest winds to \nnorthwestern SD. Deterministic and ensemble models have backed off\non the wind speeds for tomorrow afternoon. While it will be \nseasonably breezy (gusts of 35 to 45 over much of the western SD \nplains), widespread advisory level winds aren't expected. Most \nrecent CAMs have been showing light precip moving through the area\nas the front pushes through. The precip should be rain over much \nof the area, though the higher elevations of the Black Hills will \nbe cold enough for snow and could pick up some light accumulations\n(generally less than an inch). \n\nAnother wave moves through Saturday, bringing more chances for \nlight rain/snow to northeastern WY into the Black Hills and \nsouthwestern SD Saturday. Current runs indicate the greatest QPF \n(around 0.05- 0.15\") over northeastern WY into the Black Hills and\nsouthern SD. Temperatures over northeastern WY and the Black \nHills should be cold enough for snow. Snow amounts should be less \nthan an inch for most spots, though upslope enhancement will \nsupport 1 to 4\" across the higher elevations in the northern Black\nHills. Southwestern into south central SD may see snow early in \nthe morning but as the day warms up, that should transition over \nto rain. Northwest winds will also pick up again on Saturday with \ngusts reaching 40 to 45 over much of the western SD plains and \ngusts up to 55 over far northwestern SD.\n\nLooking beyond this weekend, upper level flow will transition to \nmore zonal flow early next week. This translates to highs in the \n40s to 50s and mostly dry weather.",
